<footer class="thought-notes">
<details class="thought-sources">
<summary>Sources and reading notes</summary>
<p>These entries are teaching paraphrases, not quotations. The final column links to source translations. Chapter titles and wording vary by edition; references identify the received texts.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Mengzi:</strong> <a href="https://facultysites.vassar.edu/brvannor/mengzi.html">Bryan W. Van Norden’s selected translations (1997)</a> for 1A7, 2A2, and 2A6; <a href="https://ctext.org/mengzi/gaozi-i">James Legge’s translation at the Chinese Text Project</a> for 6A2 and 6A8. A and B denote the two halves of a book.</li>
<li><strong>Laozi / Daodejing:</strong> <a href="https://ctext.org/dao-de-jing/ens">James Legge’s translation (1891)</a>, with <a href="https://scholarworks.iu.edu/dspace/items/d79d75bd-6464-4b8f-9fe8-efa076c36ea2">Robert Eno’s teaching translation, version 1.4 (2010–2025)</a>, for the uncarved block. The advice to avoid overhandling small fish explains the metaphor; the chapter itself gives the comparison without spelling out that instruction.</li>
<li><strong>Zhuangzi:</strong> James Legge’s translation at the <a href="https://ctext.org/zhuangzi">Chinese Text Project</a>. The butterfly and fish episodes invite competing interpretations; they do not simply prove that reality is illusion or settle the question of other minds. Cook Ding’s skill includes caution. This useless-tree entry follows chapter 1; the dream of Carpenter Shi and the tree is a different episode in chapter 4.</li>
<li><strong>Xunzi:</strong> <a href="https://afe.easia.columbia.edu/ps/cup/xunzi_encouraging_learning.pdf">“Encouraging Learning”</a> and <a href="https://afe.easia.columbia.edu/ps/cup/xunzi_human_nature.pdf">“Human Nature Is Evil”</a>, from <i>Sources of Chinese Tradition</i>, compiled by Wm. Theodore de Bary and Irene Bloom, 2nd ed., vol. 1 (1999), hosted by Columbia University’s Asia for Educators. These excerpts do not identify individual translators. The latter title does not mean people cannot become good: transformation through learning is central to the argument.</li>
<li><strong>Han Feizi:</strong> <a href="https://cognitionandculture.net/wp-content/uploads/1939The-Complete-Works-of-Han-Fei-Tzu-A-Classic-of-Chinese-Political-Science-ebook.pdf">W. K. Liao, <i>The Complete Works of Han Fei Tzŭ</i></a>. Passage links include PDF page locations where supported. Liao calls the weapon in the familiar spear-and-shield story a halberd. The cap-and-robe episode reports both officials being punished without resolving differences in how translators describe the penalties. The arrows and child’s-boil entries were also checked against Eirik Lang Harris’s translation in Ivanhoe and Van Norden, eds., <i>Readings in Classical Chinese Philosophy</i>, 3rd ed., pp. 376–378, used in the current course packet. Their passage links open Liao’s translation; the printed page numbers identify the course anthology. The child analogy states Han Feizi’s defense of coercive government, not an endorsement of that argument.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Compare carefully:</strong> water makes different arguments in Mengzi and Laozi. Mengzi emphasizes its natural direction; Laozi emphasizes its humility, softness, and efficacy.</p>
</details>
</footer>
</main>
